              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 10.1"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.094,
            0.797,
            0.901,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Fig. 10.1 (i), the line PQ and the circle have no common point. In this case, PQ is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "non-intersecting",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " line with respect to the circle. In Fig. 10.1 (ii), there are two common points A and B that the line PQ and the circle have. In this case, we call the line PQ a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "secant",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the circle. In Fig. 10.1 (iii), there is only one point A which is common to the line PQ and the circle. In this case, the line is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "tangent",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " to the circle."
            }
          ]
        },
